    "ZIPregularpayloads": 61,
    "main_printed_abstract_full": "Combining OpenAI’s negative solution of rational Hilbert’s tenth problem with Poonen’s established effective transfer, we obtain undecidability of rational-point existence for arbitrary smooth projective geometrically integral varieties over ℚ. We specify a homogeneous-equation input promise and reconstruct the terminating contradiction algorithm. There is also no computable uniform bound on the height of one rational point in terms of the size of the presented equations. The arithmetic input is used with two explicit corrections documented here and in the accompanying verification supplement. This is a geometric consequence of that input; neither the geometric reduction nor the base undecidability theorem is claimed as a new independent solution.",
    "companion_printed_abstract_full": "We reconstruct the non-CM arithmetic part of the five-point height argument in the pinned source [1], replacing its new unrestricted 2-adic modularity input by Pan’s published theorem at 5. The 2-adic member of the resulting system supplies the conductor at 5. The deduction is conditional on the preceding geometric and height-comparison inputs stated below; it neither certifies the entire upstream manuscript nor establishes undecidability of rational polynomial solvability.",
    "native_description_exact": "Combining OpenAI's negative solution of rational Hilbert's tenth problem, with two explicit proof corrections documented in the verification package, and Poonen's established effective transfer, this note proves undecidability of rational-point existence for arbitrary smooth projective geometrically integral varieties over Q. It specifies homogeneous-equation presentations and valid-input oracle use, and rules out a computable uniform bound on the height of one point in terms of presentation size. Dimension and all size parameters may vary. The arithmetic breakthrough is attributed to OpenAI and the geometric reduction to Poonen; no independent solution of rational Hilbert's tenth problem, new geometric reduction, fixed-dimensional theorem, many-one hardness or first-priority claim is made. AI tools were used extensively in research, drafting and adversarial verification. The preprint has not undergone conventional human peer review or refereeing at publication; no complete formal verification is claimed. The standalone height supplement explicitly states its retained geometric inputs.",
    "native_description_rendered_separately": true,
